WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.208
Some Azure CLI actions are higher impact
because they can quickly change security

2
00:00:09.288 --> 00:00:15.851
posture, data exposure, availability, or
recovery options.

3
00:00:15.931 --> 00:00:23.756
RBAC assignments, network rules, storage
access, encryption settings,

4
00:00:23.836 --> 00:00:30.399
compute configuration, Key Vault access,
logging settings,

5
00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:39.944
and data-access changes deserve stronger
guardrails than ordinary read-only queries.

6
00:00:40.024 --> 00:00:45.073
The script should make those actions
explicit.

7
00:00:45.153 --> 00:00:52.977
Azure Policy and governance controls can
provide important guardrails,

8
00:00:53.057 --> 00:01:00.125
but scripts should not treat policy as a
reason to skip validation.

9
00:01:00.205 --> 00:01:08.156
Where Azure services and deployment
workflows support what-if behavior,

10
00:01:08.236 --> 00:01:16.692
previews, dry-run concepts, policy
evaluation, diffs, or staged deployment,

11
00:01:16.772 --> 00:01:22.073
use those mechanisms as defensive review
points.

12
00:01:22.153 --> 00:01:31.240
They help reviewers understand the
effective change before a high-impact operation

13
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.591
reaches production.

14
00:01:33.671 --> 00:01:40.487
Deletion, overwrite, public exposure,
permission expansion,

15
00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:45.236
and encryption changes deserve extra care.

16
00:01:45.316 --> 00:01:53.646
A helper should require target validation,
release context, approval gates,

17
00:01:53.726 --> 00:02:02.939
operator confirmation, or change tickets
when the operation can affect production or

18
00:02:03.019 --> 00:02:05.417
sensitive resources.

19
00:02:05.497 --> 00:02:14.458
Ambiguous resource selection should fail
closed rather than let a broad command act

20
00:02:14.538 --> 00:02:17.314
on unintended resources.

21
00:02:17.394 --> 00:02:26.734
Guardrails should support idempotent
behavior, rollback thinking, and safe failure.

22
00:02:26.814 --> 00:02:34.891
A script should be designed so repeated
runs do not create uncontrolled side

23
00:02:34.971 --> 00:02:35.981
effects.

24
00:02:36.061 --> 00:02:42.750
Recovery paths should be considered before
the change begins,

25
00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:49.897
especially for identity, storage, data,
and networking changes.

26
00:02:49.977 --> 00:02:58.054
Secure Azure CLI automation makes
high-impact changes visible, reviewed,

27
00:02:58.134 --> 00:03:03.940
bounded, and recoverable where recovery is
possible.
